        <div id="how-driveways-fail" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do Concrete Driveways Fail?
          </h2>

          <p>
            From the top down, in most cases. The top quarter inch, made of the paste and fine sand troweled up when the slab was finished, is the weakest part of a driveway. Water soaks into it, freezes and expands by roughly 9 percent. Repeat that dozens of times in one winter and the surface starts shedding thin flakes, a failure called scaling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the difference between scaling and spalling?</h3>

          <p>
            Scaling is the shallow version, where thin flakes of the surface come away and leave a rough, pitted patch. Spalling is larger: whole chunks pop off, often above a corroding piece of rebar or a weak spot in the slab. Both begin with water getting into concrete that has no way to shed it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does road salt make freeze-thaw damage worse?</h3>

          <p>
            It works against the slab in two ways. Salt solutions soak deeper into concrete than plain water does, and they keep the surface wet for longer, so the saturated zone goes through more freeze-thaw cycles. Chlorides that reach reinforcing steel also start corrosion, and because rust takes up more room than the steel it replaces, the concrete cracks from the inside. That is why bridge decks and parking garages are routinely treated with silane-based repellents.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do tires and plows wear a driveway?</h3>

          <p>
            Mechanically. Wheels turned on a parked car grind the surface, heavy trucks flex thin edges, and plows and shovels chip whatever sits on top. A driveway sealer has to survive all of it, which is the main reason film-forming sealers wear out so much faster on driveways than on patios.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is a new concrete driveway most at risk?</h3>

          <p>
            Because its first winter is when young concrete is most vulnerable to salt and freeze-thaw. That is why this guide pushes to get a sealer down before the first deicer on a new slab, and why it suggests skipping chemical deicers entirely in the first year if you can, using sand for traction instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What can a sealer not fix?</h3>

          <p>
            Structural cracks from settling, heaving caused by frost in a poorly drained base, and scaling that has already happened. No sealer reverses any of those; it only slows future damage on sound concrete. If your driveway already has wide cracks or sunken panels, sort them out first. Our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">driveway crack filler guide</a> covers products for joints and hairline cracks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="penetrating-or-film" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Should You Use a Penetrating or Film-Forming Sealer on a Driveway?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Penetrating, for most concrete driveways. A silane-siloxane sealer reacts inside the pores and leaves the surface unchanged, so tires have nothing to wear through, nothing can peel, and traction stays the same. The slab also stays breathable, which matters because a driveway sits on soil that sends moisture up from below. Our roundup of <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">penetrating concrete sealers</a> compares the chemistry in more depth; this page sticks to how each type copes with a driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does a film sealer make sense on a driveway?</h3>

          <p>
            When appearance comes first. Film-forming acrylics and urethanes are the only way to get a wet look or strong color enhancement, and they hold off oil better than penetrating repellents do. On stamped, colored or exposed-aggregate driveways, plenty of owners accept the recoating cycle in return for the look. On plain broom-finished gray concrete, the argument for a film is weak.
          </p>

